      <title>Victoria Slocum RT @netbird : CrowdSec AppSec is a web app firewall that inspect</title>
      <link>https://t.me/add_twitter_list_16244885551156/85</link>
      <description>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Victoria Slocum&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;RT &lt;a href="https://twitter.com/netbird" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;@netbird&lt;/a&gt;: CrowdSec AppSec is a web app firewall that inspects every HTTP request for anything malicious.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;As soon as you spin up a fresh server, you’ll have bots hammering /.𝚎𝚗𝚟 and trying every SSH password they know.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;This is where CrowdSec comes in, blocking malicious IP addresses based on a community-curated blocklist. So when any server running CrowdSec catches an IP misbehaving, that IP gets blocked for everyone else too.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;NetBird Proxy publishes your internal services to the internet, so on top of CIDR allowlists and country blocking, you can turn on CrowdSec for each service and set it to observe or enforce.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you self-host NetBird with NetBird Proxy and CrowdSec, you can go a step further and put AppSec in front of the NetBird dashboard itself!&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;AppSec checks what the request actually asks for, which means that, for example, a probe for /.env from an IP that isn't on any blocklist will still get a 403. It covers the dashboard, its API and the sign-in endpoints, through the CrowdSec plugin for Traefik and runs on the same container as CrowdSec.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Read the full guide:&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://t.co/d4c154Z3OS" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;https://t.co/d4c154Z3OS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/victorialslocum/status/2118387183417434125" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;tweet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <content:encoded>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Victoria Slocum&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;RT &lt;a href="https://twitter.com/netbird" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;@netbird&lt;/a&gt;: CrowdSec AppSec is a web app firewall that inspects every HTTP request for anything malicious.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;As soon as you spin up a fresh server, you’ll have bots hammering /.𝚎𝚗𝚟 and trying every SSH password they know.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;This is where CrowdSec comes in, blocking malicious IP addresses based on a community-curated blocklist. So when any server running CrowdSec catches an IP misbehaving, that IP gets blocked for everyone else too.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;NetBird Proxy publishes your internal services to the internet, so on top of CIDR allowlists and country blocking, you can turn on CrowdSec for each service and set it to observe or enforce.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you self-host NetBird with NetBird Proxy and CrowdSec, you can go a step further and put AppSec in front of the NetBird dashboard itself!&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;AppSec checks what the request actually asks for, which means that, for example, a probe for /.env from an IP that isn't on any blocklist will still get a 403. It covers the dashboard, its API and the sign-in endpoints, through the CrowdSec plugin for Traefik and runs on the same container as CrowdSec.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Read the full guide:&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://t.co/d4c154Z3OS" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;https://t.co/d4c154Z3OS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/victorialslocum/status/2118387183417434125" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;tweet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</content:encoded>
